<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ahsa Oasis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Hasa, Al-Ahsa, Al-Hasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ahsa is a large oasis in the Eastern Province of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, about 60 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and one of the largest date-palm oases in the world. Its towns, chief among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Hofuf</a>, were held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from the mid-16th century, by the Banu Khalid from 1670 and by the first Saudi state from the 1790s; the Ottomans returned in 1871 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> took it in 1913. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis.jpg" alt="Al-Ahsa Oasis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: കാക്കര · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jabal_Al_Qara_Cave_-_Al_Hassa,_Saudi_Arabia_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B4%AC%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%85%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%96%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B1_%E0%B4%97%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%B9,_%E0%B4%85%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%B9%E0%B4%B8,_%E0%B4%B8%E0%B5%97%E0%B4%A6%E0%B4%BF_%E0%B4%85%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%87%E0%B4%AC%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AF_12.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
